[2026-09-04 22:44:39] [INFO] API Gateway RAW Response [prov_1779643665_n0pte]: {"id":"gen-1788561858-P77d2bYR2RFkINm61iPp","object":"chat.completion","created":1788561858,"model":"minimax/minimax-m3:free","provider":"GMICloud","system_fingerprint":null,"service_tier":null,"choices":[{"index":0,"logprobs":null,"finish_reason":"stop","native_finish_reason":"stop","message":{"role":"assistant","content":"{\"title\":\"Wine 11.0 Download for Linux and macOS (2025)\",\"meta_description\":\"Wine is free and open source compatibility layer for Linux and macOS that runs Windows apps near-native speed without emulation. Grab the official build now.\",\"slug\":\"wine\",\"focus_keyword\":\"Wine\",\"long_tail_keywords\":[\"Wine Linux compatibility layer download\",\"Wine source build Linux 64-bit\",\"Wine 11.0 stable 2025\"],\"needs_manual_input\":[\"tech_specs.homepage\",\"system_requirements.minimum.os\"],\"keyword_variations\":[\"download Wine\",\"get Wine\",\"Wine installer\",\"Wine for Linux\"],\"introduction\":[\"Wine is a free and open source compatibility layer that lets users of Unix-like systems — including **Linux**, **macOS**, and **FreeBSD** — run software that was originally written for Microsoft Windows. Despite a common misconception, **Wine** is not an emulator. The name itself is a recursive acronym for \\\"Wine Is Not an Emulator,\\\" and it works by translating Windows API calls into POSIX calls on the fly, rather than simulating an entire x86 hardware environment inside a virtual machine.\",\"Because there is no heavyweight CPU emulation running underneath the application, Windows programs launched through Wine tend to execute at near-native speeds. In some real-world workloads the memory management of the host Linux kernel actually allows the same program to feel snappier than it does on native Windows, which is one of the reasons **Wine** remains the backbone of projects like Valve's Proton and CodeWeavers' CrossOver.\"],\"tech_specs\":{\"software_name\":\"Wine\",\"file_name\":\"wine-11.17.tar.xz\",\"version\":\"11.17 Beta\",\"license\":\"Open Source (LGPL-2.1)\",\"developer\":\"WineHQ / The Wine Project\",\"homepage\":\"https://www.winehq.org/\",\"changelogurl\":\"https://www.winehq.org/announce\",\"file_size\":\"44.6 MB\",\"os_support\":\"Linux, macOS, FreeBSD, Solaris\",\"language_support\":\"English\",\"last_updated\":\"November 2025\"},\"features\":[{\"name\":\"Windows API Translation Layer\",\"description\":\"**Wine** translates Windows API calls into native POSIX calls in real time, letting Windows desktop applications run on a Unix-like host without the overhead of full hardware emulation or a hypervisor.\"},{\"name\":\"Near-Native Performance\",\"description\":\"Because no x86 CPU is being simulated, applications launched through Wine usually run at the same speed — and in some memory-heavy scenarios noticeably faster — than they would on a native Windows installation.\"},{\"name\":\"Independent, Auditable Codebase\",\"description\":\"**Wine** is built from clean, open source code that does not rely on Microsoft's proprietary components, giving users the stability and security guarantees of the underlying Linux or BSD kernel instead of the Windows runtime.\"},{\"name\":\"Native DLL Override Support\",\"description\":\"When an application needs the original Microsoft dynamic libraries to behave correctly, Wine can load them directly, while still falling back to its own built-in implementations for everything else.\"},{\"name\":\"Broad Application Coverage\",\"description\":\"The shared WineHQ AppDB catalogs tens of thousands of Windows titles — from productivity suites to AAA games — and reports which ones run flawlessly, with minor tweaks, or not at all on the current release.\"},{\"name\":\"Foundation for Proton and CrossOver\",\"description\":\"Valve's Steam Play Proton layer and the commercial CrossOver product are both built on top of Wine, meaning fixes and improvements upstream benefit gamers and professionals almost immediately.\"},{\"name\":\"Flexible Build System\",\"description\":\"**Wine** can be compiled from a small source tarball or installed through distribution packages, and supports 32-bit as well as 64-bit prefixes for legacy and modern applications side by side.\"}],\"whats_new\":[],\"system_requirements\":{\"minimum\":{\"os\":\"Linux, macOS, FreeBSD or Solaris distribution with kernel 3.x or newer\",\"processor\":\"x86_64 (also supports i386, ARM64, ARM32)\",\"ram\":null,\"disk_space\":null,\"graphics\":null},\"recommended\":{\"os\":null,\"processor\":null,\"ram\":null,\"disk_space\":null},\"_note\":\"Wine runs on most modern Unix-like systems. Specific RAM and disk requirements depend entirely on the Windows application being launched; Wine itself has no documented minimum beyond the host OS.\"},\"faqs\":[{\"question\":\"Is Wine actually an emulator?\",\"answer\":\"No. Wine is a compatibility layer that translates Windows API calls into POSIX calls at runtime. It does not emulate a CPU, a full PC, or a copy of Windows, which is why performance is typically close to native.\"},{\"question\":\"Is Wine free to download and use?\",\"answer\":\"Yes. Wine is distributed under the LGPL 2.1 license and is completely free to download, build, and use on any supported Unix-like operating system.\"}],\"installation_guide\":{\"steps\":[\"Step 1: Download the Wine 11.0 stable or 11.17 beta source tarball from the official WineHQ server using the link above.\",\"Step 2: Extract the archive with `tar -xJf wine-11.x.tar.xz` and `cd` into the resulting `wine-11.x` directory.\",\"Step 3: Install your distribution's build dependencies (gcc, make, flex, bison, libX11-devel, freetype-devel and similar packages) using your package manager.\",\"Step 4: Run `./configure --enable-win64` for a 64-bit build (add `--disable-win64` if you also need 32-bit support), then `make` and `sudo make install`.\",\"Step 5: Configure Wine the first time with `winecfg` and launch any Windows executable by running `wine program.exe`.\"],\"compatibility_notes\":\"Requires a working POSIX build environment; many Linux distributions also ship prebuilt Wine packages through their native package managers, which is the recommended path for most users.\",\"common_issues\":[\"Issue: `configure` complains about a missing X development library → Solution: install the `libX11-devel`, `libxext-devel`, `libxi-devel` and `freetype-devel` packages from your distribution.\",\"Issue: 64-bit Windows apps fail to launch on a 32-bit Wine prefix → Solution: create a 64-bit prefix with `WINEARCH=win64 winecfg` and reinstall the application into it.\",\"Issue: Fonts render as unreadable boxes → Solution: install the `corefonts` package via `winetricks` or copy the standard Windows fonts into the `~/.wine/drive_c/windows/Fonts` directory.\"]},\"tags\":[\"wine\",\"compatibility-layer\",\"freeware\",\"open-source\"],\"software_type\":\"COMPLEX_ENGINE\",\"moat_data\":{\"content\":[{\"heading\":\"Creating the right Wine prefix for a new Windows program\",\"recipe\":\"1. Open a terminal and run `export WINEARCH=win64` (or `win32` for legacy 32-bit apps).\\n2. Create an isolated prefix with `WINEPREFIX=~/wineprograms/myapp winecfg`.\\n3. Inside `winecfg` pick the Windows version that best matches the application you intend to run.\\n4. Apply the matching `winetricks` verbs, such as `dotnet48`, `vcrun2022` or `dxvk`, for that prefix only.\\n5. Install the program with `WINEPREFIX=~/wineprograms/myapp wine setup.exe` and launch it from the same prefix.\",\"why\":\"A dedicated prefix per application prevents conflicting overrides, lets you keep older apps on Windows 7 mode while modern ones run on Windows 10, and makes it trivial to nuke a broken environment without affecting anything else.\"},{\"heading\":\"Enabling DXVK and VKD3D-Proton for DirectX performance\",\"recipe\":\"1. Install `winetricks` from your distribution's package manager.\\n2. Inside the target prefix run `winetricks dxvk` for Direct3D 9/10/11, or `winetricks vkd3d` for Direct3D 12.\\n3. Verify the DLL overrides in `winecfg` under the Libraries tab — `d3d9`, `d3d10core`, `d3d11` and `d3d12` should point to the native builds.\\n4. Launch the game and check the Wine debug log for `DXVK` or `VKD3D-Proton` startup messages.\\n5. If frame pacing is uneven, add `DXVK_ASYNC=1` to the environment before launching.\",\"why\":\"Translating DirectX through Vulkan cuts CPU overhead dramatically and is the single biggest performance win for games running through Wine, often pushing frame rates above what the same title achieves in a Windows VM.\"}]},\"competitors\":[]}","refusal":null,"reasoning":null}}],"usage":{"prompt_tokens":4101,"completion_tokens":1809,"total_tokens":5910,"cost":0,"is_byok":false,"prompt_tokens_details":{"cached_tokens":128,"cache_write_tokens":0,"audio_tokens":0,"video_tokens":0},"cost_details":{"upstream_inference_cost":0,"upstream_inference_prompt_cost":0,"upstream_inference_completions_cost":0},"completion_tokens_details":{"reasoning_tokens":0,"image_tokens":0,"audio_tokens":0}}}
